  "published": "2026-11-08T20:42:44.667Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "1.13.0 keeps the license's terms on the device up to date. No API change; apps need no code change.\n\n- A renewal, a shorter end or a removed end reaches the app at its next validation or heartbeat. The server now signs the license's current end for each device, and the SDK uses it instead of the date in the license file (for every type but Trial, whose end the signed lease already carries). License.ExpirationDate shows that end, so expiry warnings use it too.\n- A trial or subscription whose file has ended but which the vendor extended is honored as soon as the server is reached; before, the app refused it from the file alone.\n- An Offline license asks the server whenever it is reachable (a 4-second check, retried after 5 minutes without an answer), so a revoke, a suspension or a renewal reaches it; with no network it keeps working from its file and cache as before.\n- Needs a LicenPro server from 2026-11-08 or later for the first point; older servers simply send no terms.\n\n1.12.1 fixes the online session when an app validates another license while it runs, for example a trial that becomes a paid license. No API change.\n\n- The session moves to the license validated last. Before, the heartbeat kept sending the first license, the new license had no session, and the app could show \"Session lost and reconnect failed: Invalid session token\" until the first session timed out.\n- The previous license's session ends on the server, so its seat is free at once.\n- Re-validating the license that is already connected keeps its session instead of reconnecting.\n\n1.12.0 adds IntelliSense documentation and removes two unused dependencies. No API or behavior change.\n\n- The package now includes XML documentation for the public API, so Visual Studio, VS Code and Rider show descriptions for LicenseClient, LicenseClientOptions, the validation results and the other public types.\n- LicenPro.SDK no longer depends on Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Binder or Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions, which it never used. If your app calls IConfiguration.Get<T>(), GetValue<T>() or Bind(), or uses IServiceCollection, and got those packages only through LicenPro.SDK, add them to your project.\n\nUpgrading from 1.8.x: also read the 1.11.0 release notes (public key built into the app, SignatureMismatch, rebuild).\n\nLanguages: .NET and VB.NET use this package. The native library for Python and other languages gets the same fix.",
